        "data": {
          "step": 3,
          "context": "The customer is not signed in. Order and account lookups need them to sign in first.",
          "message": {
            "role": "assistant",
            "content": "For wet, cold Pacific Northwest weather, I'd lean toward the **Loft Synthetic Jacket ($149.00)**.\n\nHere's the quick comparison:\n\n**Loft Synthetic Jacket — $149.00** (best pick for you)\n- 4.4 stars\n- Keeps insulating when wet — exactly what PNW drizzle demands\n- 400 g\n- Not waterproof (only water-resistant synthetics), so pair it with a rain shell\n\n**Loft Down Jacket — $199.00**\n- 4.6 stars\n- 800-fill down — warmer per gram and packs smaller (350 g)\n- But not waterproof, and down clumps and loses its loft (and warmth) when it gets wet\n\n**Bottom line:** neither jacket is waterproof, so either way add a shell. For damp conditions specifically, the synthetic's ability to stay warm when wet makes it the safer choice for the PNW. The down is the better buy for dry-cold conditions but struggles in persistent rain.\n\nIf you'd rather have waterproofing built in with insulation, the **Tundra Insulated Parka ($329.00)** combines down warmth with a waterproof shell and a -25°C rating — heavier (1,100 g) but made for deep winter and wet cold.\n\nFor either, I'd suggest adding the **Squall Pro Rain Jacket ($229.00)** or the **Squall Rain Jacket ($149.00)** as a shell. Want me to check stock in a size or run a cart quote?\n\n",
            "toolCalls": [
              {
                "id": "32faepbnr",
                "name": "reply",
                "arguments": "{\"message\":\"For wet, cold Pacific Northwest weather, I'd lean toward the Loft Synthetic Jacket ($149.00). Quick comparison: Loft Synthetic (4.4 stars) - keeps insulating when wet, 400g, not waterproof (pair with a shell). Loft Down (4.6 stars) - 800-fill, warmer per gram and packs smaller at 350g, but not waterproof and down clumps/loses warmth when wet. Since neither is waterproof, either way add a shell. For damp PNW conditions specifically, the synthetic's ability to stay warm when wet makes it the safer pick. The down is better for dry-cold conditions. If you'd rather have waterproofing built in with insulation, the Tundra Insulated Parka ($329.00) combines down warmth with a waterproof shell and a -25 deg C rating (heavier at 1100g). Want me to check a size/color or put a cart together?\"}"
              }
            ]
          },
